// Practice quiz: AI enrichment with validated outputs
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. First draft, not reviewed. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/data-pipelines/ai-enrichment-with-validated-outputs

::o1 untrusted::Why treat a model response as untrusted input? {
=It can be malformed, incomplete, or well-formed and wrong #Correct. Nothing about generated text guarantees shape or truth.
~Models are not allowed to return JSON #Not quite. They can; it still needs validating.
~Responses are encrypted #Not quite. Encryption in transit says nothing about content quality.
~Python cannot read model output without a schema #Not quite. Python reads text fine; validation is about trust.
}

::o1 schema limit::A response matches the requested JSON schema exactly. What does that show? {
~The values are correct #Not quite. A wrong category can still be valid by the schema.
~No further checks are ever useful #Not quite. Sampling and review still matter for meaning.
~The model cited its sources #Not quite. A schema says nothing about sources.
=The shape is right, not that the values are true #Correct. A schema constrains structure and types, not meaning.
}

::o2 json invalid::What does Enrichment.model_validate_json("not json") raise? {
=ValidationError, with error type json_invalid #Correct. Invalid JSON and wrong shape are both reported as ValidationError.
~json.JSONDecodeError #Not quite. Pydantic reports it through ValidationError instead.
~ValueError from the standard library #Not quite. The exception is Pydantic's ValidationError.
~Nothing; it returns None #Not quite. It raises rather than returning None.
}

::o2 bounded::Why use for _ in range(attempts) instead of while True around a model call? {
~while True is a syntax error #Not quite. It is legal; the danger is that it may never end.
~range makes the model answer faster #Not quite. It only limits the attempts.
=It bounds cost and time when responses keep failing #Correct. A fixed limit always ends in success or the reject path.
~Models accept a maximum of one retry #Not quite. The limit is your decision.
}

::o3 fake client::Which design lets a test run the enrichment step with no network? {
~Create the real client inside the function #Not quite. That ties the function to the real service.
=Pass the client as a parameter and give the test a fake with fixed responses #Correct. The step then depends only on the method it calls.
~Call the real model with a short prompt #Not quite. That still needs the network and varies between runs.
~Delete the validation during tests #Not quite. Validation is exactly what you want to test.
}
